A||||3||82-T/A||Racing and horsing around....||||11||
Z||000000||82-T/A||11-24-2000||10:39 AM||||I know I know... I'm probably the youngest here so I shouldn't be talking.. but I was reading some stuff about people racing on here and wanted to share my views.<P>I urge you all to be VERY careful when driving wrecklessly in the Fiero. Theres a time and place... obviously we can't ALL do an auto-x.. nor do we have the time.. but there are some common sense rules to racing on the streets.<P>1) Never race when there is a lot of traffic.<P>2) If your going to race at a stop light, make sure there are no blind turn-ons into your road.<P>3) If your going to race at a stop light, make sure you don't go any more than maybe 10 over the speed limit. In a 45 Zone, if you've beaten the guy to 55-60... then the chances are pretty good your car is vastly superior.. no reason to go any faster.<P>4) Make sure you only race or drive hard on roads that you know.<P><BR>Like someone on here said.. It's all fun and games until that little child chases his ball out into the street. <P>There have been numerous occasions where I've wanted to drive really hard in my car, but for SOME reason.. just decided... nah.. I'll give it a rest.. and it NEVER fails.. but there is almost always something that happened.. like a dog flying into the road.. or children playing in the street that if I HAD been racing hard, I would have killed someone. <P><BR>This one time.. I was dropping my roommate off from work (his crappy 78 Vette broke down). it was early in the morning but I had to drop him off early because I had to go to an early flight to New York for a meeting. <P>Anyway, after dropping off my roommate, I was heading to the airport and there were TWO people drag racing. They were taking up both sides of the road. There was nothing I could do.. it was either drive into the lake on the right, or head for the park fountain on the left. So I decided to nail the park founting.. so speaking of curbs.. I nailed the curb at about 45 miles an hour, and SLAMMED head on into this decorative park fountain.. man that sucker split in two...<BR>I was basically hitting a brick wall. It cracked the side and water started to pour all over my Fiero and the statuette in the center fell over... hehe.. I felt bad, but no one stopped.. so I high tailed it out of there. Apparently there was NO damage to my Fiero. The rubbermaid front panel had popped back out.<P>Anyway, drive safe.<P>Todd<P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||11
Z||000001||stimpy||11-24-2000||10:50 AM||||Uhh, that's an intersting story to the moral.  Of course it would have been more interesting if women had become widows, children had been orphaned, and blood flowed in the gutters.  But I apprecate the effort anyhow. Anything that can keep me and mine from getting taken out by someone driving recklessly is a bonus. I do find the related sory about popping over a curb at 45mph and then destroying a concrete fountain, and then being able to drive away about as believeable as Gilligan's Island.  Coconut radio anyone?||||reg||5
Z||000002||82-T/A||11-24-2000||11:07 AM||||Yeah it really sucked.... I think I damaged my ball joints. I don't think I hit the fountain at 45.. there was maybe 30 meters of grass between the curb and the fountain. <P>I braked but that apparently does nothing on wet grass. I later sold the car for exactly what I paid for it. The front end had a weird shimmy after that. But Matt Walters got it fixed.. he was even able to drive it up to Ohio from South Florida without any problems. He STILL drives it around and it's got over 160+ thousand miles on it now.. that car is a friggin BEAST!!!! I've never seen a more durable Fiero in my life... that thing was awesome. It was a total RAT... faded paint.. ripped seats.. the motor looked like it was lifted from the Titanic.. but it's original and has never been opened. Only thing that was changed was the transmission.. which was replaced with a 4.10:1 by John Altman (the guy I bought it from) <P>man that car was a BEAST... it had a leaking exhaust manifold and a hole in the muffler with no cat on it.. at 6,000 rpms.. it sounded like a Formula-1 car.<P>There was this one time I dropped the clutch at like 5.5 grand... the thing just burned rubber sitting still... I didn't even use the brakes.. the car started to spin in a circle sideways... weird. Apparently the 4.10:s supplied soo much torque to the wheel that I literally ripped the driveline absorber RIGHT off the frame.. tore the bracket in half. (got a new one from Kick hill farms)<P>That car was soo awesome.. and I miss it soo much. I suppose it's good. I used to drive really wrecklessly.. but I've tried to calm myself over the past year... the LAST thing I want is someones death over my shoulders. <P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||1
Z||000003||82-T/A||11-24-2000||11:09 AM||||But basically..... as physics would have it.. the theory states that the item with more MASS will inflict MORE damage on the apposing item regardless of force. <P>So I guess my Fiero was stronger than the park fountain. Man... that car was awesome..<P>it had NO options at all.. 4-speed manual, WS6 performance suspension, it was a hardtop.. no spoiler.. nothing.. not even power door locks. No rear defog, no trunk release.. NOTHING...<P>A REAL sports car...<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||1
